1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,224
That's a mission I wrote,

2
00:00:01,674 --> 00:00:04,820
starting on a computer that isn't mine.

3
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:08,011
Today is the last lecture of this class,

4
00:00:08,461 --> 00:00:10,480
and there's nothing new to learn in it.

5
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:13,209
Today, you simply finish it.

6
00:00:15,009 --> 00:00:16,525
You make the mission yours,

7
00:00:16,975 --> 00:00:18,362
you check it, you print it,

8
00:00:18,812 --> 00:00:23,039
and then you give it to someone.

9
00:00:24,239 --> 00:00:25,567
Don't start by typing.

10
00:00:26,467 --> 00:00:28,162
What you've built is a shape:

11
00:00:28,612 --> 00:00:31,049
go out, find a second thing, wait,

12
00:00:31,499 --> 00:00:34,124
and come home, against a clock.

13
00:00:35,024 --> 00:00:37,939
Every slot in that shape has a name that a player reads,

14
00:00:38,389 --> 00:00:40,972
and a handle that the game uses.

15
00:00:41,872 --> 00:00:44,359
So write your story into the slots first.

16
00:00:46,159 --> 00:00:49,139
Here's mine: a navigation beacon has gone dark,

17
00:00:49,589 --> 00:00:53,094
its keeper is missing, and a storm is ten minutes out.

18
00:00:54,294 --> 00:00:58,537
There's one rule: change names and sentences, and leave every key.

19
00:00:59,437 --> 00:01:01,259
The square brackets are mine,

20
00:01:01,709 --> 00:01:03,634
and the round brackets are the game's.

21
00:01:04,534 --> 00:01:07,252
So my beacon is still called derelict underneath,

22
00:01:07,702 --> 00:01:10,372
and no player will ever see that word.

23
00:01:12,172 --> 00:01:14,709
I change the win line, and the lose line.

24
00:01:15,609 --> 00:01:17,683
Then an objective, where the number in it

25
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:20,471
must still agree with the line under it.

26
00:01:21,371 --> 00:01:22,470
And then a reading.

27
00:01:23,370 --> 00:01:26,809
That comes to fifty-eight changes, and not one key.

28
00:01:28,609 --> 00:01:32,856
Then the script, which has five places, all between quote marks.

29
00:01:33,756 --> 00:01:36,594
It's the first pair of quotes on each line.

30
00:01:37,494 --> 00:01:40,618
The second pair is the side and the roles, so leave it.

31
00:01:41,818 --> 00:01:45,263
Lint says clean, because words can't break the chain,

32
00:01:45,713 --> 00:01:48,022
and that's why we change them first.

33
00:01:49,222 --> 00:01:51,734
Now, two files you haven't opened.

34
00:01:52,634 --> 00:01:56,112
This one is what the server shows in its list of missions,

35
00:01:56,562 --> 00:01:58,766
and you change two lines in it.

36
00:01:59,666 --> 00:02:02,720
And there's one rule here that matters more than any other today:

37
00:02:03,170 --> 00:02:06,385
no hyphen, and no colon, in these two values.

38
00:02:07,285 --> 00:02:11,679
The game reads this file for every mission, before it shows anything,

39
00:02:12,129 --> 00:02:14,395
and a hyphen here stops the game.

40
00:02:15,295 --> 00:02:17,099
Not your mission, the game.

41
00:02:18,899 --> 00:02:22,189
Lint reads this file now, and it stops you with an error.

42
00:02:23,089 --> 00:02:24,025
Doctor doesn't read it.

43
00:02:24,925 --> 00:02:27,860
So keep to plain words, commas and full stops,

44
00:02:28,310 --> 00:02:31,399
and run lint after you change it.

45
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:35,297
Then the ship, on line seventy.

46
00:02:36,197 --> 00:02:39,822
Change the word between the quote marks, and nothing else.

47
00:02:40,722 --> 00:02:44,776
If I break this line, my ship is Artemis again, and nothing tells me.

48
00:02:45,676 --> 00:02:47,128
I find out when I play.

49
00:02:48,328 --> 00:02:50,665
You don't have to do this.

50
00:02:51,565 --> 00:02:53,107
But if a key bothers you,

51
00:02:53,557 --> 00:02:56,354
there's one way, and only one:

52
00:02:56,804 --> 00:03:00,613
Replace, across the whole folder, with both buttons on.

53
00:03:01,513 --> 00:03:03,858
Read the list first: there are nine places,

54
00:03:04,308 --> 00:03:08,048
and look at the last one, which is in the script, on my card.

55
00:03:08,948 --> 00:03:11,124
So it's replace all, save all, and lint.

56
00:03:11,574 --> 00:03:13,593
It's clean, and it works.

57
00:03:15,393 --> 00:03:16,764
Now for the other way.

58
00:03:17,664 --> 00:03:20,166
I change it by hand, all through the fact sheet.

59
00:03:21,066 --> 00:03:23,659
Lint says clean, and it's broken.

60
00:03:24,559 --> 00:03:27,696
The tender never comes, and the game can't be won.

61
00:03:28,596 --> 00:03:30,628
The address on the card

62
00:03:31,078 --> 00:03:33,898
is the one place lint can't follow a key.

63
00:03:35,098 --> 00:03:38,197
Everybody in this class has a folder called My Mission.

64
00:03:39,097 --> 00:03:40,887
So if you send yours to a classmate,

65
00:03:41,337 --> 00:03:43,361
it lands on top of theirs.

66
00:03:44,261 --> 00:03:47,135
That's why it gets its own name, with no spaces.

67
00:03:48,035 --> 00:03:50,687
Nothing inside the files knows the folder's name.

68
00:03:51,587 --> 00:03:54,728
Only I do, in every command from now on.

69
00:03:55,928 --> 00:03:57,185
There are three checks,

70
00:03:57,635 --> 00:03:59,959
and each one sees what the others can't.

71
00:04:00,859 --> 00:04:03,568
Lint asks, is the writing right?

72
00:04:04,468 --> 00:04:07,473
Doctor asks, is everything this mission needs on this computer?

73
00:04:08,373 --> 00:04:10,255
Look for the block with my folder's name,

74
00:04:10,705 --> 00:04:13,787
and then the last two words: zero problems.

75
00:04:14,687 --> 00:04:16,578
Doctor never reads my writing.

76
00:04:18,378 --> 00:04:20,001
And then comes the play.

77
00:04:20,901 --> 00:04:24,101
There's my ship, my station, and my beacon.

78
00:04:24,551 --> 00:04:27,170
Then the skiff, the wait, and the tender.

79
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:30,249
Then home, and my win line.

80
00:04:31,149 --> 00:04:32,354
Both logs are empty.

81
00:04:33,254 --> 00:04:36,984
And then I read every sentence once more, as a reader.

82
00:04:38,184 --> 00:04:40,361
My mission is also a text,

83
00:04:40,811 --> 00:04:42,199
and it takes one command.

84
00:04:43,099 --> 00:04:47,466
It has a title, it says player, which means leave out the machinery,

85
00:04:47,916 --> 00:04:49,467
and it asks for a PDF.

86
00:04:50,367 --> 00:04:52,472
That makes two files, in a new folder.

87
00:04:54,272 --> 00:04:55,890
This is for a friend who reads,

88
00:04:56,340 --> 00:04:58,530
and not for a friend who plays,

89
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:01,288
because it's the whole plot, in order.

90
00:05:02,188 --> 00:05:05,082
And there's one for me, called the bible.

91
00:05:05,982 --> 00:05:08,439
It goes beat one, beat two,

92
00:05:08,889 --> 00:05:10,709
with reached from, and leads to.

93
00:05:11,609 --> 00:05:13,462
If a step's in the wrong place here,

94
00:05:13,912 --> 00:05:16,635
a Then line points at the wrong place.

95
00:05:17,535 --> 00:05:19,691
There's one thing it gets wrong:

96
00:05:20,141 --> 00:05:23,687
it says win, false, and lose, false.

97
00:05:24,587 --> 00:05:27,109
My two sentences are fine.

98
00:05:27,559 --> 00:05:30,480
It's the printing that isn't.

99
00:05:31,680 --> 00:05:34,501
A mission is a folder,

100
00:05:34,951 --> 00:05:37,215
so to share it, I send the folder.

101
00:05:38,115 --> 00:05:40,681
First I take out what isn't the mission.

102
00:05:41,581 --> 00:05:44,926
That's my documents, because they're the plot,

103
00:05:45,376 --> 00:05:47,460
and the scratch folder and the logs,

104
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:49,518
which come back by themselves.

105
00:05:50,418 --> 00:05:51,663
That leaves seven files,

106
00:05:52,113 --> 00:05:54,776
and it's all seven, not only the two I wrote.

107
00:05:55,676 --> 00:05:57,763
Then the folder itself gets zipped,

108
00:05:58,213 --> 00:06:00,680
and it comes to seven kilobytes.

109
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:04,297
My friend puts the folder where mine is.

110
00:06:05,197 --> 00:06:06,976
There's one check: open it,

111
00:06:07,426 --> 00:06:10,390
and the files are right there, not another folder.

112
00:06:11,290 --> 00:06:14,537
Then it's four lines: is the tool new enough,

113
00:06:14,987 --> 00:06:16,802
and if it isn't, update it.

114
00:06:17,702 --> 00:06:19,866
Then fetch the libraries this mission names,

115
00:06:20,316 --> 00:06:24,093
because even if they play every week, theirs may be older than mine.

116
00:06:24,993 --> 00:06:26,310
And then start it.

117
00:06:27,510 --> 00:06:28,783
And that's the class.

118
00:06:29,683 --> 00:06:31,882
The rubric on the page is what done means,

119
00:06:32,332 --> 00:06:34,584
and you can tick every line yourself.

120
00:06:35,484 --> 00:06:37,083
Then do the first exercise:

121
00:06:37,533 --> 00:06:40,284
send it to one person, and don't help them.

122
00:06:42,084 --> 00:06:44,549
Class 2 puts people in your story.
